WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.780
Though they don't really have much to say
themselves, the zombies in The Walking Dead

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.279
definitely have a story.

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:14.030
From production decisions to the makeup and
lore, here's the truth about those mysterious walkers.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:19.590
Walkers in The Walking Dead are an ever-present
threat to every character on the show, but

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.702
they're a relatively manageable one.

00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:26.652
It's established early on in the series that
the walkers generally adhere to classic tropes.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:30.820
For instance, the undead hordes usually only
travel at a walking pace.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.829
There's a reason the show isn't called The
Jogging Dead.

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:38.431
Also, despite the fact that zombie bites cause
the victims to turn into the undead, there

00:00:38.431 --> 00:00:43.020
are solutions to this dilemma -- such as when
Rick was able to prevent Hershel from turning

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.251
by severing his leg above the zombie bite.

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:51.590
When a person turns into a walker, they lose
almost all remnants of their former self.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:56.830
High-level thinking is unavailable to them,
and while they might remember small things

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.422
like how to open doors, that's about it.

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:06.400
Their driving force is an insatiable hunger
for the flesh of living things, a drive that

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.920
can't be quelled unless the brain of the walker
is destroyed.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.640
One of the biggest questions that's plagued
fans is how exactly the zombie outbreak started.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.079
Unfortunately, neither the original comics
or the television series provide concrete answers.

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:27.821
Though he's been reluctant to disclose the
virus' root cause in his works, Walking Dead

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:34.159
author Robert Kirkman apparently unveiled
the genesis of the zombie apocalypse on Twitter.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:40.170
According to the now-deleted Tweet, the virus
started from some sort of "space spore."

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:45.220
This explanation immediately raised a few
eyebrows, and considering Kirkman's history

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.780
of inserting jokes about aliens as the source
of the zombie virus, that particular answer

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.010
seemed a bit suspect.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:58.470
Though the space spore explanation caused
some curiosity and debate, it would soon be

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.979
confirmed that Kirkman was once again playing
a prank on his fans.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:07.370
Shortly after he deleted his initial tweet
confirming space spores started the zombie

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:12.590
outbreak, Kirkman again took to Twitter explaining
that he'd only been joking.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.900
Ultimately, The Walking Dead's lack of an
outbreak cause is most likely a tribute to

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.100
legendary filmmaker George Romero, who never
fully explained why the zombies were reanimating

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.680
in his original Living Dead films.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.230
One of the key creatives behind the show is
Greg Nicotero, a long-time Hollywood veteran

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:36.200
who has more practical experience with zombies
than just about anyone in the world.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.430
Nicotero got his start working with zombies
when he joined the crew of George Romero's

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.680
1985 film Day of the Dead.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:50.000
While there, he worked with legendary monster
makeup artist Tom Savini to help create undead horrors.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.571
Years of experience have given Nicotero innumerable
opportunities to hone his craft.

00:02:55.571 --> 00:03:00.800
Soon after wrapping up work on Day of the
Dead, Nicotero dipped his toes into many other

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.290
iconic horror franchises, such as Evil Dead
II, Nightmare on Elm Street, Halloween, Texas

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:12.077
Chainsaw Massacre, The Hills Have Eyes, and
Hostel, among others.

00:03:12.077 --> 00:03:15.890
But Nicotero didn't just limit himself to
horror films.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:22.250
He also did makeup for Spy Kids, The Adventures
of Sharkboy and Lavagirl 3-D, Transformers,

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.660
and the Kill Bill movies.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.569
Throughout the series, the walkers in The
Walking Dead are fairly consistent beings.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:35.220
They don't have any wants outside of flesh,
and their means of finding food are limited

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.420
to clumsily biting what prey they manage to
stumble upon.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:46.623
Aside from the sheer power presented by a
horde, walkers are simple creatures with no intelligence.

00:03:46.623 --> 00:03:52.550
However, while the walkers never stray too
far from being mindless, hunger-driven creatures,

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.040
there are some notable examples where they
display some amount of intelligence.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.221
Specifically, the walkers in season one are
notably more resourceful than the walkers

00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:03.504
in later seasons.

00:04:03.504 --> 00:04:08.080
For example, Morgan's undead wife attempts
to turn the doorknob to their home before

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.819
mindlessly bashing at the front door.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.430
More impressively, some walkers can be seen
using tools like rocks to break into buildings,

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:21.119
demonstrating that they're capable of more
advanced problem-solving skills than just

00:04:21.119 --> 00:04:23.689
running and biting victims.

00:04:23.689 --> 00:04:28.740
Series creator Robert Kirkman provides some
insight in a Reddit "Ask Me Anything", saying,

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:35.703
"Fresher zombies, which there were more of
in season one, can do more than older, more rotted zombies."

00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:41.754
In other words, the walkers in season one
are newly minted and at their prime, whereas

00:04:41.754 --> 00:04:48.766
in later seasons, they've started to rot and
lose their physical and intellectual capabilities.

00:04:48.766 --> 00:04:52.979
Bringing the undead to life for TV is no small
feat.

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:57.740
An underappreciated element of The Walking
Dead's walkers is the excessive amounts of

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:01.982
blood used to make them all the more terrifying
and realistic.

00:05:01.982 --> 00:05:05.717
Special effects designer Greg Nicotero told
CBS News:

00:05:05.717 --> 00:05:12.409
"The Walking Dead probably uses up the most
amount of blood that we've ever had on any

00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:13.409
show.

00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:19.039
I would say per episode, we're usually somewhere
in the 20 to 30 gallons range."

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:22.059
But not all fake blood is created equal.

00:05:22.059 --> 00:05:28.180
Nicotero explains that the blood is made on
set, and depending on the scene, varied recipes

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.158
are used for different effects.

00:05:30.158 --> 00:05:35.529
"We have dark zombie blood, we have dried
blood, we have coagulated blood.

00:05:35.529 --> 00:05:40.539
If you have a shirt like my shirt, which is
dark, and you want to see the blood on it,

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:44.439
it has to be brighter, otherwise it would
just fade in with the color."

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:50.979
Nicotero even revealed a surprising ingredient
in his blood concoction to get the right consistency.

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:57.244
In an interview with Allure, Nicotero admitted
using K-Y Jelly as a thickening agent for the blood.

00:05:57.244 --> 00:06:03.889
Besides makeup and all other hands-on aspects
of creating walkers, there also exists the

00:06:03.889 --> 00:06:10.029
difficulty of doing all of that work justice
by finding the best way in which to film them.

00:06:10.029 --> 00:06:14.729
In an interview with Digital Trends, Greg
Nicotero noted that The Walking Dead was initially

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:17.869
conceptualized in various shooting formats.

00:06:17.869 --> 00:06:21.879
During the production of the first season,
the series was tested using Panavision, a

00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:27.589
RED digital camera, and even 35 millimeter
film before the showrunners ultimately settled

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:29.484
on the Super 16 format.

00:06:29.484 --> 00:06:34.619
During a test using Panavision, a walker was
placed under green trees, resulting in the

00:06:34.619 --> 00:06:37.259
walker's makeup looking green.

00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:42.639
Though color correction could be used to solve
the issue, Nicotero and The Walking Dead production

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:48.360
team decided to use Super 16, the medium that
would result in the best-looking shot before

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.229
color correction and editing.

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:55.990
For each episode of The Walking Dead, the
necessity of special effects is a given.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.879
There's likely to be some effects applied
to background shots in order to make a given

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:02.429
shooting location look different.

00:07:02.429 --> 00:07:07.659
Characters are also sometimes enhanced by
airbrushing blemishes, adding sweat, or making

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:11.139
bloody, oozing wounds more visceral.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:15.809
Any scene with a walker getting stabbed with
a weapon will have a prop extended to go through

00:07:15.809 --> 00:07:17.400
the shambling corpse.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:23.331
For the episodes that require a hefty amount
of effects, it sometimes takes quite a while to finish them.

00:07:23.331 --> 00:07:27.189
Showrunner and executive producer Angela Kang
remarked:

00:07:27.189 --> 00:07:32.459
"From the start of an episode getting locked
[...] through the end of the VFX process — it's

00:07:32.459 --> 00:07:37.129
a weeks-long process — on the short end,
like a month.

00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:41.661
But really you want, ideally, at least a couple
months to do it."

00:07:41.661 --> 00:07:46.959
Because The Walking Dead is filmed in Georgia,
the actors, crew, and all of the extras are

00:07:46.959 --> 00:07:50.000
sometimes subjected to extreme weather.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.594
Temperatures in the summer can reach well
into the 90s, with humidity making everything feel hotter.

00:07:55.594 --> 00:08:00.309
During the colder months, temperatures on
the set can sink below freezing.

00:08:00.309 --> 00:08:04.909
These intense filming conditions have created
several complications for the special effects

00:08:04.909 --> 00:08:06.550
masters of the show.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:11.849
According to makeup artist Toby Sells, traditional
materials used in climate-controlled sound

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:15.000
stages, such as gelatin, melt in the summer
sun.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.449
"The conventional wisdom led us to find a
really big stage, which we did here at Pinewood,

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:25.224
and build multiple sets for the various settings
that take place throughout the script"

00:08:25.224 --> 00:08:30.360
Some of the workarounds used to keep extras
and actors more comfortable are pretty creative.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.770
For example, David Morrisey — who played
the Governor in the show's third and fourth

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:38.579
seasons — had ice packs sewn into his leather
jacket.

00:08:38.579 --> 00:08:43.760
But nevertheless, keeping everyone cool and
safe is always a challenge.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.629
As costume designer Eulyn Womble explained,
during the hotter days of filming:

00:08:48.629 --> 00:08:51.810
"Fighting the humidity is the hardest part.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.860
We have to keep the zombies hydrated.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.389
We have a medic on staff.

00:08:55.389 --> 00:09:01.873
If you see somebody who looks dehydrated,
you do whatever you have to do to keep them comfortable."

00:09:01.873 --> 00:09:05.899
So what happens when the temperatures drop?

00:09:05.899 --> 00:09:06.899
According to Womble:

00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:11.480
"In winter, we can layer the zombies properly,
and we have a Snuggie wrangler.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.599
He literally runs out and puts Snuggies on
the zombies when it's cold."

00:09:16.599 --> 00:09:21.629
With each new season, the walkers become more
noticeably more decomposed.

00:09:21.629 --> 00:09:27.769
As the apocalypse rages on, it's only natural
that the undead would continually fester away.

00:09:27.769 --> 00:09:32.750
However, Greg Nicotero has expressed that
while the walkers may become more visibly

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:39.172
decayed, there's a line in the sand that the
visual effects and makeup teams are unwilling to cross.

00:09:39.172 --> 00:09:43.870
Though The Walking Dead has been more inclined
to show sagging skin and rotting flesh in

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:49.870
newer seasons, the walkers will never decompose
to the point of becoming walking skeletons.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.399
In an interview with SlashFilm, Nicotero stated:

00:09:52.399 --> 00:09:57.379
"[Showrunner] Scott [M. Gimple] has one very
specific thing that he is conscious of, which

00:09:57.379 --> 00:09:59.889
is that it can't be a Ray Harryhausen.

00:09:59.889 --> 00:10:02.180
It can't be a walking skeleton.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.069
It always has to be muscles.

00:10:05.069 --> 00:10:08.740
It always has to be something that is motivating
the movement."

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:14.720
So while the walkers may grow more ragged
over time, Walking Dead fans can rest assured

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:19.620
that they'll never wind up watching a modern-day
version of Jason and the Argonauts.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:24.070
Instead, they'll get to appreciate all the
new ways that walkers will decompose within

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:26.536
the show's self-imposed limit.

00:10:26.536 --> 00:10:32.420
The most commonly associated word with undead
people walking around is "zombie."

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.220
However, the term is notably absent from The
Walking Dead.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.540
Shedding some light on this discrepancy in
a conversation with Conan O'Brien, Robert

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:45.029
Kirkman explained why the characters never
say the word.

00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:49.870
According to Kirkman, zombie fiction simply
doesn't exist in the world he created.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:50.920
He explains:

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.020
"We wanted to avoid that notion of, 'Hey,
why doesn't that character just shooting that

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.514
zombie in the head because it saw all those
movies I saw.'"

00:10:58.514 --> 00:11:04.480
As such, the works of George Romero, the creator
of the Living Dead films that established

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.189
many tropes in the modern zombie genre, are
absent from The Walking Dead's world.

00:11:09.189 --> 00:11:14.129
Kirkman further explains that without Romero's
works, the people within this fictional realm

00:11:14.129 --> 00:11:18.992
have no conceptualization of conventions and
rules outlined in zombie media.

00:11:18.992 --> 00:11:20.139
He states:

00:11:20.139 --> 00:11:25.284
"It felt like having people not use that word
would kind of separate it from that, make

00:11:25.284 --> 00:11:27.448
it a little bit more clear."

00:11:27.448 --> 00:11:32.680
There have always been a few particularities
that make The Walking Dead seem a bit far-fetched.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:38.759
For example, as the main cast treks through
the United States, they seem to regularly

00:11:38.759 --> 00:11:41.879
be in short supply of firearm ammunition.

00:11:41.879 --> 00:11:48.220
But according to the Brookings Institute,
over 400 million guns are in the United States,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:52.439
likely with ammunition in vast quantities,
as well.

00:11:52.439 --> 00:11:57.230
Additionally, the show's survivors are frequently
shown driving years after the start of the

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:03.110
apocalypse, despite regular gasoline degrading
in less than a year, according to J.D. Power.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:08.399
However, there's been an even more persistent
trend that seems to run counter to what one

00:12:08.399 --> 00:12:12.540
may expect in a real-life zombie apocalypse
situation.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.889
In The Walking Dead, almost all of the walkers
are skinny people.

00:12:16.889 --> 00:12:21.310
This doesn't quite vibe with the Centers for
Disease Control and Prevention's data for

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:28.430
Georgia in 2019 -- which recorded that self-reported
obesity was at 33.1%.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:34.110
Given this disparity between real-life demographics
and the population of the undead, series creator

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.128
Robert Kirkman offered an answer.

00:12:36.128 --> 00:12:40.310
In a letter column for The Walking Dead #111,
he wrote:

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:44.910
"Frankly, it's hard to ADD to big people to
make them look like zombies.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:52.127
Keep in mind, to make those walkers look real,
you have to build stuff, on top of real people.

00:12:52.127 --> 00:12:56.449
[...] You start adding to people my size,
and well [...] we start to look a bit too

00:12:56.449 --> 00:12:58.851
padded, if that makes sense."

00:12:58.851 --> 00:13:04.420
Though Rick and his band of survivors typically
use the word "walker" to refer to each undead

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:07.320
specimen, other names are offered throughout
the series.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:12.626
For instance, the denizens of Alexandria refer
to the walking corpses as "roamers."

00:13:12.626 --> 00:13:18.220
Others include "rotters," as heard in season
five, and "biters," as they're called by the

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.599
residents of Woodbury in season three.

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:25.889
And of course, there's also "stinker," the
nomenclature used by Connie, Kelly, Luke,

00:13:25.889 --> 00:13:28.828
Magna, and Yumiko in seasons nine and ten.

00:13:28.828 --> 00:13:34.339
These varied alternatives to the word zombie
coincide with Kirkman's longtime tribute to

00:13:34.339 --> 00:13:38.920
Romero, who specially avoided the Z-word in
his Living Dead films as well.

